From f0a5ab7114dbadb22018022f96f183d40c7908df Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: wisp <wisp@localhost>
Date: Wed, 25 Nov 2026 20:08:12 +0300
Subject: [PATCH] PS4 (Wisp): WebGPU for pages, on Dawn

WPE has WebCore's WebGPU (bindings, InternalAPI, the Implementation layer) but nothing
under it: the Implementation layer calls WebKit's fork of the C API (WebGPU.framework,
Metal, Apple's ports only). This ports the Implementation layer to Dawn's standard
webgpu.h, for builds with HAVE_WEBGPU_IMPLEMENTATION (the PS4 web process's tree; the
app links Dawn, on Vulkan, in its GPU web process).

- WebGPUDawn.h: string views both ways, CompletionHandlers boxed as callback userdata,
  and DawnEventPump: callbacks use WGPUCallbackMode_AllowProcessEvents, and while one
  is owed a helper thread posts wgpuInstanceProcessEvents to the thread that asked
  (WebKit's schedule-work function), at most every 2 ms.
- Every Impl file on Dawn's names and structs: *_INIT descriptors (bind group layout
  entries keep BindingNotUsed), WGPUTexelCopy*, pass timestamp writes, the max draw
  count chain, WGPUOptionalBool depth writes; AddRef/Release in WebGPUPtr; the IsValid
  checks become null checks; WebKit-only calls (XR, IOSurface swap chains, external
  texture updates) give null or do nothing.
- Buffer::copyFrom writes into Dawn's mapped range.
- The device: errors a page doesn't catch, and a lost device, in the log
  (WTFLogAlways, "WebGPU (Dawn): ..."; the first 40 errors, then every 500th;
  WISP_WEBGPU_LOG=0 turns the errors off); a spent encoder or pass is a cached invalid
  object.
- WebGPU::create() asks the app, wisp_webgpu_usable(); a weak definition here says no,
  for a process without Dawn.
- Canvases: GPUCanvasContext::create() gives GPUCanvasContextWisp off Cocoa (with
  coordinated graphics). Its textures are the presentation context's own Dawn textures;
  present() copies the frame into a mappable buffer (at most two in flight), and the
  mapped pixels become a Skia NativeImage that the canvas's layer shows through
  GraphicsLayerContentsDisplayDelegateCoordinated and that the compositor integration
  paints into the canvas's ImageBuffer.
